 "external_reference_note": "C++ drivers, probes and their outputs live outside this repository under ../oracle/ so that no C++ is committed here. The Rust full-sweep probe is kept there too because it reads the multi-megabyte sweep tables, which are not committed. Recorded, not re-verified by the in-repo gate.",
 "target_verification": {
  "revision": "bc9cc12514c768385ce121d6ca4bb710fe1983c4",
  "oracle_build": "Linux x86_64 Release install /ceph/ibmi/abi/oliver/opt/openms4-release-bc9cc12-c19e494-174b576 on ibminode06 (conda-forge GCC 14.4.0, libstdc++ 6.0.36, glibc 2.39, core flags -O3 -DNDEBUG -std=gnu++23 -mssse3 -ffp-contract=off). ../oracle/a2-textfmt-linux compiles the pinned NumericFormatting.h and reproduces the install's D, S and F rows byte for byte; both runs reproduce, and an -O0 control is byte-identical. Superseded build: product-sdk libOpenMS core 4fdec46, Debug, AppleClang 21, libc++, macOS arm64, where 4fdec46..bc9cc12 changes appendNumeric only in a libc++ long-double branch (commit 74526a8).",
  "nan_sign_coverage": "The corpus holds exactly three NaN bit patterns: 7ff8000000000000 (D and the F/G sections), fff8000000000000 (D row 92) and the float 7fc00000 (S row 420). Row 92 is the only sign-bit NaN anywhere, so number(-NaN, n) is pinned at n in {0,1,2} and ostream(-NaN, p) at p in {6,15}. No sign-bit NaN float is pinned at all; every other digit count, precision and the float overload are pinned by the companion sweep ../oracle/a2-textfmt-nan-sweep, registered in SOURCE_PROVENANCE.json and captured against the same Release install: six NaN shapes x both signs x double and float over 22 digit counts and 16 precisions, 912 sign-bearing glibc rows of which 0 print a sign that disagrees with the argument's sign bit, and 24 toStr rows all printing NaN. The port's spelling is therefore measured rather than generalised, in both directions: glibc keeps the sign everywhere it was swept, and NumericFormatting discards it everywhere.",
